<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meidum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meidum is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on the desert edge west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, about 100 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Its pyramid, completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> in the 26th century BCE, was converted from a step pyramid into a true pyramid and later lost much of its outer casing. Nearby mastaba tombs yielded the painted Meidum Geese and the statues of Rahotep and Nofret.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meidum.jpg" alt="Meidum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurohito · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meidoum03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Meidum, also written Maydum or Maidum, is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>. Its ancient Egyptian name, Mr(y)-Jtmw, means beloved of Atum. The site holds a large pyramid and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> mastabas. Because of its unusual appearance the pyramid is called el-heram el-kaddaab, the false pyramid, in Egyptian Arabic. In its ruined state it stands 65 metres high. Its entrance, on the north side, is about 20 metres above the present ground, and a descending passage 17 metres long leads to a horizontal passage, a vertical shaft and a corbelled burial chamber that was unlikely to have been used for a burial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The pyramid is thought to be the first of three main pyramids built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>, although it has been argued that construction began under Huni, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Third Dynasty. It was built in three phases, numbered E1 to E3 by the archaeologist Ludwig Borchardt. E1 was a step pyramid like the Djoser Pyramid; E2 added a layer about 5 metres wide and raised the steps from five to seven. In E3 the steps were filled with limestone casing to make a true pyramid, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with smooth sides.</p><p>The work had flaws. The outer layer was founded on sand rather than rock, and the platforms of the inner steps sloped outward, which weakened the structure, and the pyramid partly collapsed in antiquity. Franck Monnier and others believe the collapse came only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. The mortuary temple was found intact under the rubble, and the complex appears never to have been finished: there is no valley temple, the burial chamber has raw walls and the mastabas were never used. The instability is a likely reason for the change of slope from 54 to 43 degrees at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>'s Bent Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Expedition investigated the pyramid in 1799, when it already had its present three steps. John Shae Perring excavated it in 1837 and Lepsius in 1843. Flinders Petrie later found the mortuary temple, which faces east, and worked out the original proportions: 1100 cubits round and 175 cubits high, the same ratio as the Great Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. Borchardt studied the area in 1920, Alan Rowe in 1928 and Ali el-Kholi in the 1970s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meidum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its monuments defined Egypt for the world: the Step Pyramid of Saqqara, Sneferu's three pyramids at <b>Meidum</b> and Dahshur, the Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, with which the fully developed pyramid style was reached, Khafre's second pyramid and, in traditional thinking, the Great Sphinx of Giza, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
